<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Panchala</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 BCE – 350 BCE (approx.) · capital Ahichchhatra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful Vedic kingdom east of the Kurus, between the Ganges and the Yamuna, later one of the sixteen mahajanapadas of early Buddhist texts.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Formed in the Later Vedic period from an alliance of several clans, among them the Krivis, the Panchalas settled east of the Kuru lands. Vedic texts pair Kuru and Panchala as the heart of the Vedic world, and Panchala kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keshin-dalbhya">Keshin Dalbhya</a> appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a> as patrons of sacrifice and ritual learning.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Panchala king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravahana-jaivali">Pravahana Jaivali</a> appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> instructing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> on the doctrine of rebirth, a sign of the court's intellectual standing. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> divided the kingdom into a northern half with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahichchhatra">Ahichchhatra</a> and a southern half centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampilya">Kampilya</a>. Iron tools and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and barley farming supported a growing population in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware</a> settlements. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a> a Panchala princess.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the 6th century BCE Panchala had become a sangha governed by leading clans. It was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanda-empire">Nanda Empire</a> in the 4th century BCE and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a>. Local kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a> line later issued coins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahichchhatra">Ahichchhatra</a> in the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/panchala.jpg" alt="Panchala" width="320" height="245"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Vedic_Culture_(1100-500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 BCE – 350 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ahichchhatra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Keshin Dalbhya, Pravahana Jaivali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vedic Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Panchala, Panchala Mahajanapada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-725&amp;focus=panchala" aria-label="Panchala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6707 1187 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Panchala lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7027 1443L7253 1492"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7027 1443h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7027 1443h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-725&amp;focus=panchala">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

